# Try SystemOne with Laya

This standalone notebook installs SystemOne, downloads the Laya model, and
asks typed questions about application data. You do not need to clone the
SystemOne repository.

The model download is approximately 1.6 GB. CPU inference adds roughly 1.6 GB
of resident memory and may take several seconds per query. This notebook uses
the CPU so it works without CUDA-specific setup.

## Install SystemOne

`Mix.install/2` downloads SystemOne and its dependencies from Hex. Livebook
caches compiled dependencies, so subsequent sessions generally start faster.

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:system_one, "~> 0.1.0"},
    {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ]
)
```

## Download and verify Laya

The bundle is stored in your user cache rather than beside the notebook. The
installer verifies pinned SHA-256 checksums and reuses a complete existing
bundle. The first evaluation can take several minutes, depending on the
connection.

```elixir
model_dir = Path.join([System.user_home!(), ".cache", "system_one", "models", "laya"])

{:ok, _started} = Application.ensure_all_started(:req)

progress = fn
  {:download, path} -> IO.puts("Downloading #{path}")
  {:verified, path} -> IO.puts("Verified #{path}")
  {:complete, path} -> IO.puts("Bundle ready at #{path}")
end

case SystemOne.Installer.install(SystemOne.Models.Laya,
       destination: model_dir,
       progress: progress
     ) do
  {:ok, ^model_dir} ->
    Kino.Markdown.new("✅ Laya is installed and verified at `#{model_dir}`.")

  {:error, reason} ->
    raise "Laya installation failed: #{inspect(reason)}"
end
```

## Start a CPU serving

Loading happens asynchronously. The second call waits until the model is
ready and returns the status, including the serving process identifier.
Re-evaluating this cell reuses the already-started notebook instance.

```elixir
name = SystemOne.Livebook

case SystemOne.start_link(
       name: name,
       model: SystemOne.Models.Laya,
       model_dir: model_dir,
       execution_providers: [:cpu]
     ) do
  {:ok, _pid} -> :ok
  {:error, {:already_started, _pid}} -> :ok
end

{:ok, status} = SystemOne.await_ready(name, timeout: 120_000)
status
```

## Ask several question types

One query can contain choice, ordered-score, and yes/no-probability questions.
The `:noul` value is a probability, not a boolean. The score is a fractional
expected zero-based level.

```elixir
alias SystemOne.{Query, Question}

query =
  Query.new(%{message: "The production server is down again! Please help now."}, [
    Question.new(:sentiment, :choice, "How does this message feel?",
      criteria: ["positive", "neutral", "negative"]
    ),
    Question.new(:urgency, :score, "How urgent is this?",
      criteria: ["can wait", "today", "right now"]
    ),
    Question.new(:spam, :noul, "Is this spam?")
  ])

{:ok, result} = SystemOne.query(name, query)
result
```

## Inspect the useful fields

```elixir
for {id, answer} <- result.answers, into: %{} do
  {id,
   %{
     value: answer.choice || answer.score || answer.noul,
     confidence: answer.confidence,
     probabilities: answer.probabilities,
     metadata: answer.metadata
   }}
end
```

Try changing the message, instructions, and criteria, then reevaluate the last
two cells. A query must contain 1–64 questions. Choice and score questions
accept 1–20 options; noul always has two model options.

Each question is encoded into at most 512 tokens. Instructions and options
share a 192-token header budget, and each option is initially capped at 48
tokens. State uses the remaining space. Excess text is silently truncated;
`result.usage.input_tokens` reports the post-truncation total. SystemOne does
not limit input bytes before tokenization, so applications should reject
unreasonably large untrusted requests themselves. Question IDs should also be
unique because answers are keyed by ID.

## Handle lifecycle errors

Applications should not assume every call succeeds. A production caller
should handle loading, stopped, failed, and invalid-query results explicitly:

```elixir
case SystemOne.query(name, query) do
  {:ok, result} -> {:served, result}
  {:error, {:invalid_query, message}} -> {:bad_input, message}
  {:error, {:unavailable, reason}} -> {:temporarily_unavailable, reason}
end
```

For CUDA, complete the repository's GPU guide first, rebuild Ortex with CUDA
support, and change the providers to `[:cuda, :cpu]`. A `:ready` status alone
does not prove that the CUDA provider loaded.
